== 12. God Has Chosen You to Reveal His Secrets To

Twelfthly, consider that you#super[+] are the only persons in all the world whom God has chosen to reveal His secrets to. “No longer do I call you#super[+] servants, for a servant does not understand what his master is doing. But I have called you#super[+] friends, because everything I have learned from My Father I have made known to you” (John 15:15). Everything that God the Father had imparted to Christ as mediator, to be revealed to His servants, He made known to His disciples as to His bosom friends. Christ loves His people as friends, He treats them as friends, and He opens His heart to them as friends. There is nothing in the heart of Christ that concerns the internal and eternal welfare of His friends that He does not reveal to them. He reveals Himself, His love, His eternal goodwill, the mysteries of faith, and the secrets of His covenant to His friends. Christ loves to share with His friends things that are not commonly and popularly known. Christ will reveal the secrets of His mind, the secrets of His love, the secrets of His thoughts, the secrets of His heart, and the secrets of His purposes, to all His bosom friends. Samson could not hide his mind, his secrets, from Delilah, though it cost him his life (Judg 16:15–17); and do you#super[+] think that Christ can hide His mind, His secrets, from those for whom He has laid down His life? Surely not. O sirs! Christ is

- a universal friend,
- an omnipotent friend, an almighty friend. He is called Almighty no fewer than thirty times in the book of Job; He can do above all that words can express and beyond all that minds can grasp.
- He is an omniscient friend.
- He is an omnipresent friend.
- He is an unfailing friend.
- He is an independent friend.
- He is an unchangeable friend.
- He is a watchful friend.
- He is a tender and compassionate friend.
- He is a close and faithful friend.

Therefore, He cannot help opening and unbosoming Himself to all His bosom friends. To be reserved and secretive is against the very law of friendship. Faithful friends are very free in imparting their thoughts, their minds, their secrets, to one another. A real friend counts nothing worth knowing unless he makes it known to his friends. He lays open his greatest and most inward secrets to his friends. Job calls his friends “inward friends,” or the men of his secrets (Job 19:19). All Christ’s friends are inward friends; they are the men of His secrets. “His secret is with the righteous” (Prov 3:32); that is, His covenant and fatherly affection, which is hidden and secret from the world. He who is righteous in secret, where no man sees him, is the righteous man to whom God will impart His closest secrets, as to His dearest bosom friend. It is only to a bosom friend that we will unbosom ourselves. “The secret of the #smallcaps[Lord] is with those who fear Him, and He reveals His covenant to them” (Ps 25:14).

Now, there are three sorts of divine secrets:

(1) First, #emph[there are secrets of providence], and these He reveals to the righteous and to those who fear Him (Ps 107:43; Hos 14:9). The prophet Amos speaks of these secrets of providence: “Surely the Lord #smallcaps[God] does nothing without revealing His secret to His servants the prophets” (Amos 3:7). Micah knew the secret of the Lord concerning Ahab, which neither Zedekiah nor any other of the false prophets knew. “And the #smallcaps[Lord] said, ‘Shall I hide from Abraham what I am about to do?’” (Gen 18:17). The destruction of Sodom was a secret that lay in the bosom of God; but Abraham being a bosom friend, God imparts this secret to him (vv. 19-21). Abraham was a friend, a faithful friend, a friend in a special way (Jas 2:23); and therefore God makes him a member both of His court and of His council. Oh, how greatly God stoops to His people! He speaks to them as a man would speak to his friend; and there are no secrets of providence that may be for their advantage that He will not reveal to His faithful servants. As all faithful friends have the same friends and the same enemies, so they freely share their secrets with one another; and so it was between God and Abraham.

(2) Secondly, #emph[there are the secrets of His kingdom], and these He reveals to His people. “The knowledge of the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven has been given to you#super[+], but not to them…At that time Jesus declared, ‘I praise You, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because You have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children’” (Matt 13:11; 11:25). “Let us not think,” says Jerome, “that the gospel is in the words of Scripture, but in the sense; not in the outside, but in the marrow; not in the leaves of words, but in the root of reason.” Augustine humbly begged God that, if it were His pleasure, He would send Moses to him to interpret some of the more obscure and intricate passages in his book of Genesis. There are many choice, secret, hidden, and mysterious truths and doctrines in the gospel which Christ reveals to His people, and to which this poor, blind, ignorant world is a stranger. There are many secrets wrapped up in the plainest truths and doctrines of the gospel, which no one can effectually open and reveal but the Spirit of the Lord, who “searches all things, even the deep things of God” (1 Cor 2:10). There are many secrets and mysteries in the gospel that all the learning and labor in the world can never give a man insight into. There are many who know the doctrine of the gospel, the history of the gospel, who are utter strangers to the secrets of the gospel. There is a secret power, a secret authority, a secret efficacy, a secret prevailing power, a secret goodness, a secret sweetness in the gospel, that none experience but those to whom the Lord is pleased to impart gospel secrets. “Seal the law among my disciples” (Isa 8:16; 29:11–12). The law of God is to wicked men a sealed book that they cannot understand (Dan 12:9–10). It is like blotted paper that they cannot read. Just as a private letter to a friend contains secret matter that no one else may read, because it is sealed, so the law of grace is sealed up under the privy seal of heaven, so that no man can open it or read it except Christ’s faithful friends, to whom it is sent. The whole Scripture, says Gregory, is only one entire letter sent from the Lord Christ to His beloved spouse on earth. The rabbis say that there are four keys that God keeps at His belt: 1) the key of the clouds; 2) the key of the womb; 3) the key of the grave; 4) the key of food. I may add a fifth key that is at His belt, and that is the key of the Word, the key of the Scripture; and none can turn this key but the One “who holds the key of David. What He opens no one can shut, and what He shuts no one can open” (Rev 3:7).

O sirs! God reveals Himself and His mind and will and truth to His people in a more friendly and intimate way than He does to others. “He replied, ‘The mystery of the kingdom of God has been given to you#super[+], but to those on the outside everything is expressed in parables’…He replied, ‘The knowledge of the mysteries of the kingdom of God has been given to you, but to others I speak in parables, so that though seeing, they may not see; though hearing, they may not understand’” (Mark 4:11; Luke 8:10). Great doctors, and profound scholars, and deep-studied but unsanctified divines may know much of the doctrines of the gospel, and commend the doctrines of the gospel much, and dispute much for the doctrines of the gospel, and glory much in the doctrines of the gospel, and take a great deal of pains to dress and trim up the doctrines of the gospel with the flowers of rhetoric or eloquence—though it is much better to present truth in her native plainness than to hang her ears with counterfeit pearls (the Word, without human adornments, is like the stone #emph[Garamantides] that has drops of gold in itself, enough to enrich the believing soul). Yet the special, spiritual, powerful, and saving knowledge of the doctrines of the gospel is a secret, a mystery, indeed, a hidden mystery to them (Rom 16:25; 1 Cor 2:7).

Chrysostom compares the mysteries of Christ, with regard to the wicked, to a written book that the ignorant man can neither read nor spell. He sees the cover, the leaves, and the letters, but he does not understand the meaning of what he sees. He compares the mystery of grace to a dictated letter, which an uneducated person looks at but cannot read, cannot understand. He knows it is paper and ink, but the sense, the matter, he does not know, he does not understand. So unsanctified persons, however learned they are, and though they may perceive the bark of the mystery of Christ, yet do not perceive, do not understand, the mystery of grace, the inward sense of the Spirit in the blessed Scriptures. Though the devil is the greatest scholar in the world, and has more learning than all the men in the world have, yet there are many thousands of secrets and mysteries in the gospel of grace that he does not know really, spiritually, feelingly, efficaciously, powerfully, thoroughly, savingly.

Oh, but now Christ makes Himself, His mind, His grace, His truth, known to His people in a more clear, full, intimate, and friendly way. “For You, O #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts, the God of Israel, have revealed this to Your servant” (2 Sam 7:27). So you#super[+] read it in your Bibles; but in the Hebrew it is this: “Lord, You have revealed this to the ear of Your servant.” Now, the emphasis lies in the words “to the ear,” which are left out in your Bibles. When God makes Himself known to His people, He reveals things to their ears, as we are accustomed to do with a friend who is intimate with us: we speak a thing in his ear. There is many a secret that Jesus Christ speaks in the ears of His servants, which others never come to know. “For God, who said, ‘Let light shine out of darkness,’ made His light shine in our hearts to give us the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ” (2 Cor 4:6).

The six distinct gradations in this scripture are worthy of our most serious consideration. Here is:

First, knowledge;

Second, the knowledge of the glory of God;

Third, the light of the knowledge of the glory of God;

Fourth, shining;

Fifth, shining in our hearts; and,

Sixth, shining in our hearts in the face of Jesus Christ.

And so you see that the Lord reveals the secrets of Himself, His kingdom, His truth, His grace, His glory, to the saints.

(3) Thirdly, there are #emph[the secrets of His favor], the secrets of the special love that He bears to them, the secret purposes of His heart to save them. And these are those great secrets, those “deep things of God,” which none can reveal “except the Spirit of God” (1 Cor 2:10–11). Now these great secrets, these deep things of God, God reveals to His people by His Spirit. “But God has revealed them to us by the Spirit. The Spirit searches all things, even the deep things of God. For who among men knows the thoughts of man except his own spirit within him? So too, no one knows the thoughts of God except the Spirit of God. We have not received the spirit of the world, but the Spirit who is from God, that we may know what God has freely given us” (1 Cor 2:10–12). Now what are the things that God has freely given us, but our election, calling, justification, sanctification, and glorification? And why has God given us His Spirit, but that we should know “what God has freely given us” (v. 12)? Some understand by “secret” in the 25th Psalm a particular assurance of God’s favors, by which happiness is secured to us, both for the present and for the future. They understand by “secret” the sealing of the Spirit, the hidden manna, the white stone, and the new name written on it, which no one knows but he who has it. And the words “He reveals His covenant to them” seem to mean as much (Ps 25:14); for what greater secret can God impart to His people than opening the covenant of grace to them in its freeness, fullness, sureness, sweetness, suitableness, and everlastingness, and sealing up to them His good pleasure and all the spiritual and eternal blessings of the covenant? Those who love and serve the Lord will be of His cabinet council; they will know His soul secrets, and be admitted into a very gracious intimacy and friendship with Him. “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the one who loves Me. The one who loves Me will be loved by My Father, and I will love him and reveal Myself to him. Judas (not Iscariot) asked Him, ‘Lord, why are You going to reveal Yourself to us and not to the world?’ Jesus replied, ‘If anyone loves Me, he will keep My word. My Father will love him, and We will come to him and make Our home with him’” (John 14:21–23). God and Christ will keep house with them, and make known the secrets of Their love to those who are observant of Their commands. And so you see that the saints are the only persons to whom God will reveal the secrets of His providence, the secrets of His kingdom, and the secrets of His love. Christ came out of the bosom of His Father, and He opens all the secrets of His Father only to His bosom friends.

Now what an exceedingly high honor it is for God to open the secrets of His love, the secrets of His promises, the secrets of His providences, the secrets of His counsels, and the secrets of His covenant to His people! Tiberius Caesar thought no man fit to know his secrets. And among the Persians none but noblemen, lords, and dukes might be made partakers of state secrets. They regarded secrecy as a godhead, a divine thing, as Ammianus Marcellinus affirms. But God has put such honor on all His saints as to make them lords and nobles, and the only privy statesmen in the court of heaven. The highest honor and glory that earthly princes can put on their subjects is to share their greatest secrets with them. Now this high honor and glory the King of kings has put on His people. “The secret of the #smallcaps[Lord] is with those who fear Him, and He reveals His covenant to them” (Ps 25:14). It was a high honor to Elisha that he could tell the secrets that were spoken in the king’s bedroom (2 Kgs 6:12). Oh, what an honor it must be, then, for the saints to know the secrets that are spoken in the presence chamber of the King of kings!

Now I appeal to the very consciences of all who fear the Lord: is it not a just, fair, righteous, and necessary thing that the people of God should freely and fully lay open all the secrets of their hearts before the Lord, Who has so highly honored them as to reveal the secrets of His providence, kingdom, and favor to them? Yes, I appeal to all serious and sincere Christians: is it not against the light and law of nature, and against the law of love and the law of friendship, to be reserved and secretive, indeed, to hide our secrets from Him Who reveals His greatest and His choicest secrets to us? And if it is, why then do you#super[+] not lay open in secret all your secret sins, and secret needs, and secret desires, secret fears, etc., to Him who sees in secret? You#super[+] know that all secrets are to be shared only in secret. None but utter fools will share secrets on a stage or before many.
